Q: Improving structure of a Java base conversion program A: <p>Welcome to StackOverflow! Good luck with this, your first question.</p>
<p>I'll start with some comments unrelated to what you asked. Well, at least my understanding of what you asked.</p>
<p>Thank you for mentioning that you know about the built in methods, but you want to do this as a learning exercise, so you didn't use them.</p>
<p>I find the inclusion of the user interface methods distracting. If you want us to critique the conversion methods without regard to the user interface, for StackOverflow,  you might want to replace the user interface methods with a test method:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static void test () {
    steps (&quot;ABC&quot;,16,10);
    steps (&quot;9AB&quot;,16,8);
    steps (&quot;37&quot;, 7, 2);  // error
    steps (&quot;111000110101&quot;, 2, 10);
}
and so on. 
</code></pre>
<p>You might want to read about making a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example">Minimal Reproducible Example</a> . On the other hand, you might want us to critique the user interface. I assumed you do not. More recommended reading includes the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/tour">Tour</a> and pages it links to.</p>
<p>Now, on to your conversion code. I don't have an answer for improving your object-oriented design. But, there are Java basics I would like to point out.</p>
<p>I see, after getting the &quot;source&quot; number and base from the user,  you convert to a common, intermediate base. You then convert from the common, intermediate base to the representation in the &quot;destination&quot; base. That is a good idea.</p>
<p>But, here is the surprise: The intermediate base you used isn't decimal! It's binary. See the tutorial on <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/java/nutsandbolts/datatypes.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java Primitive Types</a> .</p>
<p>Consider a pair of lines like these:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>int foo = 123;
System.out.println (foo);
</code></pre>
<p>The compiler converts <code>123</code> to binary: <code>0000000001111011</code>. When handling the <code>println</code> method, without being informed otherwise, the system assumes the output is to be in &quot;human readable&quot; form. It converts the value in <code>foo</code> to decimal, and to characters the output device can handle. So, it looks to you as if it is in decimal.</p>
<p>Your code works because the math you used will work regardless of which base is used as an intermediary.</p>
<p>Now, I have a question for you:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Why do  you have methods <code>fromDec</code> and <code>fromDecSteps</code>?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Why do you have methods <code>toDec</code> and <code>toDecSteps</code>?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Except for the <code>System.out.println</code> methods, one of a pair does what the other does.</p>
<p>If I may go off-topic again, I would like to comment on the use of <code>StringBuilder</code> in code like this:</p>
<pre><code>static String fromDec(int n, int b) {
        if (n == 0)
            return &quot;0&quot;;
        StringBuilder r = new StringBuilder();
        while (n &gt; 0) {
            r.insert(0, vc(n % b));
            n /= b;
        }
        return r.toString();
    }
</code></pre>
<p>The line <code>r.insert(0, vc(n % b));</code> would cause shifting of the current contents prior to insertion, or copying current contents to a new <code>StringBuilder</code> , leaving a space at the beginning.  Consider using the <code>r.append(vc(n % b));</code> instead. Then, use<code>return r.reverse().toString()</code>;</p>
<p>By the way, you can estimate the size of the <code>StringBuilder</code>  you will need before creating it:</p>
<pre><code>double size = (int)( Math.ceil( Math.log((double) n ) / Math.log ((double) b)) ) + 1; 
StringBuilder = new StringBuilder (size); 
</code></pre>

Q: How to access all values with a specified key regardless of its level in a multidimensional array A: <p>If the values that you want to access are always &quot;primitive&quot; data types (leaf nodes), just try with <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.array-walk-recursive.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_walk_recursive</code></a>.</p>
<p>If you might want to access a subarray (non-primitive data type / not a leaf node) by its key, then <code>array_walk_recursive()</code> will not suffice.</p>
<pre><code>$output = [];
array_walk_recursive($input, function ($value, $key) use (&amp;$output) {
    if ($key === 'sub_key') {
        $output[] = $value;
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>array (size=2)
  0 =&gt; string 'val' (length=3)
  1 =&gt; string 'val' (length=3)
</code></pre>

Q: Event listener for when element becomes visible? A: <p>If you just want to run some code when an element becomes visible in the viewport:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function onVisible(element, callback) {
  new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) =&gt; {
    entries.forEach(entry =&gt; {
      if(entry.intersectionRatio &gt; 0) {
        callback(element);
        observer.disconnect();
      }
    });
  }).observe(element);
  if(!callback) return new Promise(r =&gt; callback=r);
}
</code></pre>
<p>When the element has become visible (even slightly) the intersection observer calls <code>callback</code> and then destroys itself with <code>.disconnect()</code>.</p>
<p>Use it like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>onVisible(document.querySelector(&quot;#myElement&quot;), () =&gt; console.log(&quot;it's visible&quot;));
</code></pre>
<p>or like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await onVisible(document.querySelector(&quot;#myElement&quot;));
console.log(&quot;it's visible&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>If you want the callback to trigger when the element becomes <strong>fully visible</strong> then you should change <code>entry.intersectionRatio &gt; 0</code> to <code>entry.intersectionRatio === 1</code>.</p>
<p>If the element is <strong>already visible</strong> when you call <code>onVisible</code>, then the callback will fire immediately.</p>
<p>Related: If you want to <em>immediately</em> get a <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> for whether the element is <em>currently</em> visible, use <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/53942619/11950764">this code</a> (change the <code>intersectionRatio</code> to suit your requirements).</p>

Q: Is there a way to use mixin classes as classes instead of as values? A: <p>I'll assume that by &quot;a <em>real</em> class&quot;, you mean a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/class" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>class</code> declaration</a>.</p>
<p>In TypeScript, a <code>class</code> declaration creates a value (just as it does in JavaScript), but it also implicitly creates a type of the same name. This type models instances of the class.</p>
<p>That's why code like <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/#code/MYGwhgzhAEBiD29oG9oEsIKQXmgFwCcBXAU2gF8AoS4eAOwj2gDNEAuORaXOkgd07wAFAEpKAenHRpMmQD18ATwAOZedABuYEKSA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a> is legal:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>class Foo { isFoo = true }

const foo: Foo = new Foo()
//         ^ type    ^ value
</code></pre>
<p>The above is roughly equivalent to <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#code/MYewdgzgLgBAYiEMC8NgBsCGEIwN4wCWECSqUATgK4CmMAvgFBQCeADnaSjAJKRSYwwGgBV2NADysOIAGbxEAPkaNQ-GLMQAuBWRhgaAd10AKAJSMA9JZi27dgHoxpdRzABumdLSA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>const Foo = class { isFoo = true }
type Foo = InstanceType&lt;typeof Foo&gt;

const foo: Foo = new Foo()
//         ^ type    ^ value
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Armed with this knowledge, you may realize that one way to make <code>EightBitSprite</code> act like a &quot;<em>real</em> class&quot; is by <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#code/MYGwhgzhAEDKAOAnAlgFwKbQN4ChrQDswBbTAXmgHJK9oAPaCgBloE9HoXbgB7AiVIgCuwVD0QAKIqQBc0ASgIBzAJTZa+VAAtkEAHTTyhEuloBfHBZwAzIQVHI+cYGBDoAPABUAQpEzo6DAIAExgCdAB3aAk9WLBEJQg5MAJWAG0AXTUyAD5sMxyJXwh0OR8--jo7OQwDQIFYMx5AmgxDA8AA8tY1TbNfj0DAKOEorTo+4FJ0lESrerbfbCgqye9yxXK1WKwA9am6aANgg8KJuJSuIA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">declaring its instance type explicitly</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>const EightBitSprite = Scale(Sprite)
type EightBitSprite = InstanceType&lt;typeof EightBitSprite&gt;

const mapOfEightBitSprites = new Map&lt;string, EightBitSprite&gt;()
//                                           ^ this is now legal
</code></pre>
<p>Another way is to <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?#code/MYGwhgzhAEDKAOAnAlgFwKbQN4ChrQDswBbTAXmgHJK9oAPaCgBloE9HoXbgB7AiVIgCuwVD0QAKIqQBc0ASgIBzAJTZa+VAAtkEAHTTyhEuloBfHBZwAzIQVHI+cYGBDoAPABUAQpEzo6DAIAExgCdAB3aAk9WLBEJQg5MAJWAG0AXTUyAD5sMxyJXwh0OR8-NVx8RHRUIUQCaFBIGFgXEGRlaACg0OhizCr8aAB9CHajAEYcDXlattd0CXHFuQIhYgAjdERK2c0dfTGJjhW3Wath6CVa+QmJFTWN7cR1K+ra+sbtXT1jxYu5ksMwA9CDoABhHjEeA8ErQMCESJNcBQaDWRDQ6DaTAIFAYFEtAA0ODB0AiaC02K0mAAssg6J1nIsEfB4B0djIcM00QBRZBKLSobxoPFofyBdAhVr3MUYSpWXj8VDQYhgeAAeWs-MFwtFSHFMAo4SitPV7gUnSUROgOqFItQcvQhRUpPB7w9nq93s9AD1qbpoIGCDwom4lK4gA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">use a <code>class</code> declaration to define <code>EightBitSprite</code></a>. There's no need for a third <code>RealEightBitSprite</code> class:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>class EightBitSprite extends Scale(Sprite) {}
</code></pre>
<p>Either way, you'll now have a type named <code>EightBitSprite</code> which contains both properties from the base <code>Sprite</code> class and the mixin.</p>
